| Chunking Strategy         | Description                                                             | Best Use Case            | Advantages                    | Disadvantages                         |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Fixed Size Chunking       | Split text into equal-sized chunks (e.g., 500 characters or 300 tokens) | Small documents          | Simple and fast               | May split sentences or ideas          |
| Recursive Chunking        | Split by paragraphs → sentences → words if needed                       | General RAG applications | Preserves context better      | Slightly slower                       |
| Sentence Chunking         | One or more complete sentences per chunk                                | FAQ, articles            | Natural language boundaries   | Chunk sizes vary                      |
| Paragraph Chunking        | Each paragraph becomes one chunk                                        | Books, reports           | Maintains logical sections    | Some paragraphs are too large         |
| Semantic Chunking         | Split based on meaning using embeddings                                 | High-quality RAG         | Better retrieval accuracy     | Computationally expensive             |
| Token-Based Chunking      | Split by token count (e.g., 512 tokens)                                 | LLM-based applications   | Fits model context limits     | Ignores document structure            |
| Sliding Window Chunking   | Overlapping chunks (e.g., 500 tokens with 100-token overlap)            | Question answering       | Reduces information loss      | More storage and duplicate embeddings |
| Document Section Chunking | Chunk by headings/subheadings                                           | Manuals, technical docs  | Keeps topic consistency       | Requires structured documents         |
| Markdown Chunking         | Split by Markdown headers                                               | README, documentation    | Preserves hierarchy           | Only works for Markdown               |
| HTML Chunking             | Split by HTML tags                                                      | Websites                 | Maintains page structure      | Requires HTML parsing                 |
| Code Chunking             | Split by functions, classes, methods                                    | Source code RAG          | Keeps code logically grouped  | Language-specific parsing needed      |
| Table Chunking            | Split tables row-wise or section-wise                                   | CSV, Excel, databases    | Preserves tabular information | Large tables require careful handling |


